WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, episode 39.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.720
It's July 21st, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.380
From the launch pad to the trading floor,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.320
here's everything that moved today.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.720
Vandenberg's 50th launch of 2026

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.820
shows Falcon 9 reuse, sustaining Cadence

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.100
while Starship reties its next V3 test flight.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.440
Remember the Falcon Cadence discussion from yesterday.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.080
The Western range at Vandenberg Space Force Base

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.380
supported its 50th launch of 2026 today,

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.320
marking the site's busiest year on record for orbital missions.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.320
SpaceX flew its 50th orbital mission of the year

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.760
from the California site on a Falcon 9

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.920
carrying another batch of Starlink satellites from SLC4E.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.680
The same booster lifted off successfully

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.100
after a last-second engine start aboard the previous day

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.300
and had already completed 22 prior flights.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.680
Ground operations coordinated multiple vehicles

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.960
and payloads through shared infrastructure

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.360
while range crews maintained full safety

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.820
and telemetry coverage.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.920
Recovery teams tracked the first stage landing

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.640
on the drone ship and collected the payload fairings

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.540
for later reuse.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.720
That rapid turnaround on existing hardware

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.400
moves the open question of annual launch rate ceiling forward

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.060
by demonstrating sustained tempo without new range assets.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.820
From Falcon reuse on the ground,

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.720
the next vehicle applies the same reuse logic

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.060
at higher altitudes.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.680
Starship last appeared here yesterday.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.040
SpaceX is preparing the next V3 integrated test flight

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.880
after the recent countdown aboard.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.320
The vehicle now carries upgraded tankage,

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.860
avionics and thermal protection aimed

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.420
at higher payload capacity.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.720
Ground teams reviewed data from the prior attempt

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.240
and set a July 23 target window

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.620
that will collect fresh heat shield performance

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.480
and engine throttling measurements.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.560
The flight profile continues the program's iterative approach

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.020
to flight software and structural changes

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.340
ahead of orbital refueling demonstrations later this year.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.640
This test moves the open question of full

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.780
and rapid reuse cadence forward

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.820
by validating the V3 hardware changes in flight.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.460
While Starship iterates in Texas,

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.380
another Falcon payload demonstrates immediate

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.620
on-orbit utility.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.420
A Falcon 9 is scheduled to carry Northrop Grumman's

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.220
first two armed satellite servicing spacecraft

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.640
on an upcoming mission.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.360
The dual manipulator system is designed to capture

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.200
and service legacy satellites

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.500
that lack dedicated servicing ports.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.220
Mission planners expect the demonstration

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.820
to validate capture sequences and tool use operations

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.020
in microgravity for the first time.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.640
Success would directly extend the operational life

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.420
of existing on-orbit assets

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.900
without requiring new satellite builds.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.420
Servicing existing satellites pairs naturally

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.700
with delivering new crews to the station.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.160
NASA has scheduled briefings

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.240
for the crew 13 Dragon Mission planned for September.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.620
The flight will rotate crew members

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.500
to the International Space Station,

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.840
deliver new science payloads

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.340
and return samples from prior expeditions.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.280
Training for the mission incorporates lessons

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.820
from earlier Dragon flights

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.180
to refine rendezvous procedures.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.320
Dragon operations continue their steady crew

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.080
and cargo rotation cadence

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.260
that supports both NASA and commercial station needs.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.200
While Dragon supports people in orbit,

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.760
Starlink expands connectivity on the ground.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.760
Starlink has begun offering residential

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.860
and commercial service in Seychelles

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.960
after regulatory approval and gateway installation.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.640
Terminals are now available for immediate activation

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.220
in previously underserved island areas

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.420
using existing satellite passes.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.480
The rollout delivers low latency links

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.160
without requiring additional orbital assets

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:58.740
or constellation changes.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.980
Starlink's terrestrial growth sits alongside

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.120
the larger musk compute buildout.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.860
Cursor reported that pairing a high performance planner model

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.980
with a low cost executor model

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:14.040
reduced large project inference costs by roughly one eighth.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.080
The same agents rebuilt

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:17.900
ESAQP from scratch

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.000
and passed the complete test suite

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.980
without any human written code.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.860
Elon Musk stated that SpaceX engineering data

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.240
will feed the next two trillion parameter

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.460
GROC training run.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.800
Researchers also published a sandbox bypass

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.880
that affects cursor, codex,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.560
and Gemini CLI through crafted inputs.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.720
Observers on X noted the rapid Falcon 9 turnaround

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.600
between the abort and the successful 50th

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.440
Vandenberg launched the next day.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.040
Several accounts highlighted the booster's steadily climbing

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.480
reuse count while community members shared coastal photos

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.480
of the Starlink deployment plume.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.320
One thread discussed how the Northrop Grumman robot arms

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.640
could extend satellite life once demonstrated on orbit.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.740
Worth keeping an eye on,

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.640
Musk's data center project faces accusations

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.300
of bypassing clean air regulations in multiple states,

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.640
reports site installation of unpermitted turbines

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:15.980
to meet power demand,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.080
and regulators are reviewing whether facilities

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.500
violated permitting timelines before operations began.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.700
Resolution would require either retroactive approvals

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.380
or operational adjustments.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.320
On the AI front,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.400
the planner executor split and SpaceX data

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.000
feeding the next GROC run

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.100
show how the compute thread continues to draw directly

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.900
from flight hardware experience.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:43.520
The engineering angle here starts with the raw cost

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.260
of silicon, electricity, and training tokens

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.080
that produce the cursor agents.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.820
That floor sits at a tiny fraction

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.900
of the engineering hours once required

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.520
to write and debug an equivalent database engine from scratch.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.720
The idiot index measures the multiplier

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:01.920
between that token and watt floor

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.780
and the delivered working code.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.820
Each successful test suite pass collapses the multiplier

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.820
by removing human review loops

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.460
because the planner selects architecture

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.240
while the cheaper executor writes the implementation.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:19.060
The system trades a modest increase in planning tokens

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.260
for an eightfold reduction in total inference spend.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.160
That tradeoff holds only when the planner's higher capability

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.140
steers the executor away from dead end paths

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:33.160
and assumption now validated on a non-trivial code base.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.280
The next measurable step is whether the same split scales

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.340
to larger safety critical systems

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.560
where verification cost dominates.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.040
If the pattern holds, the engineering bottle next shifts

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.760
from writing code to specifying requirements

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.620
and running exhaustive tests,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.700
exactly the constraint SpaceX already faces

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.380
when scaling Raptor production

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.140
or Starship flight software.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.700
Cursor's planner executor split mirrors the division

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.600
between Starship's flight computers

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.360
and ground test stands

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:08.100
where high fidelity simulation runs on expensive hardware

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.300
while lower fidelity checks run on commodity clusters.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.320
In both domains, the goal is to drive the idiot index

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.660
toward one by letting raw compute

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.800
replace repeated human iteration.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.520
Continued measurement of token cost

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.300
versus delivered functionality

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.720
will show how quickly that gap narrows

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.740
just as repeated hardware reuse

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.540
already compresses marginal cost per flight.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:33.440
And a quick market note,

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:40.500
SPCX closed at $127.67, up 6.1%.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.340
Before we go, watch for the July 23rd Starship V3 window

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:48.140
and any fresh heat shield data it may return.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:52.000
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:59.080
SPCX is trading at $127.67, up 6.1%.

